.016754150390625, -0.0191497802734375, -0.0133819580078125, -0.0260009765625, 0.01421356201171875, -0.02581787109375, -0.00879669189453125, 0.05596923828125, 0.0141448974609375, -0.003429412841796875, 0.01548004150390625, 0.00922393798828125, 0.01447296142578125, 0.021697998046875, -0.0186767578125, -0.0222625732421875, 0.02203369140625, 0.01166534423828125, 0.0182342529296875, -0.00217437744140625, -0.00336456298828125, -0.00998687744140625, 0.0160064697265625, 0.0146636962890625, 0.006099700927734375, 7.873773574829102e-05, 0.016754150390625, -0.01273345947265625, 0.0186309814453125, -0.0244140625, -0.0030918121337890625, -0.004062652587890625, 0.013031005859375, 0.02630615234375, 0.006389617919921875, -0.0016002655029296875, 0.02398681640625, 0.03143310546875, -0.0005307197570800781, 0.0194549560546875, -0.0216827392578125, 0.02545166015625, 0.0167083740234375, -0.034149169921875, 0.018707275390625, -0.01473236083984375, 0.0006918907165527344, 0.0185699462890625, -0.051116943359375, -0.003143310546875, -0.0009860992431640625], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "District heating networks are pivotal for achieving low-carbon heat supply, but their efficient and reliable operation remains challenging, particularly for complex, meshed topologies subject to nonlinear hydraulic and thermal constraints, time-varying renewable inputs, and thermal storage. Traditional optimization approaches\u2014most commonly mixed-integer nonlinear programming (MINLP) solvers and local, gradient-based methods\u2014often struggle to efficiently address these problems at scale. Although much of the existing literature focuses on steady-state optimization or dynamic simulation, a robust and scalable global optimization approach for large, realistic multi-period networks remains an open research challenge.\n\n**Objectives and Main Contributions:**  \nThis paper addresses the gap in scalable, high-fidelity global optimization for district heating networks. Our main objectives are: (i) to develop an improved network modeling framework that retains both hydraulic and thermal nonlinearities, (ii) to design and detail novel algorithmic enhancements for global optimization suited to large, meshed network instances with storage and multi-period operation, and (iii) to systematically benchmark our approach against established MINLP and local optimization solvers. Our principal contributions include:  \n- Quantitative characterization of the applicability of the steady-state approximation in district heating.\n- The development of specific branch-and-bound and symmetry-exploiting techniques for scalable optimization, with procedural details.\n- Release of reproducible synthetic and real-world test sets, with explicit randomization protocol and full data access.\n- In-depth analysis of performance, uncertainties, and known limitations, with practical guidance for model selection.\n\n**Modeling Framework and Assumptions:**  \nOur network optimization model adopts a stationary, nonlinear formulation. Each discrete time step denotes a steady-state snapshot, governed by the following core equations:\n\n- **Hydraulic equations:** The Darcy-Weisbach relation models the pressure drop,  \n  \\[\n  \\Delta p_{ij} = f_{ij}(Q_{ij}, T_{ij}) = \\lambda_{ij} \\frac{L_{ij}}{D_{ij}} \\frac{1}{2} \\rho(T_{ij}) \\left( \\frac{Q_{ij}}{A_{ij}} \\right)^2\n  \\]\n  where:\n  - \\( \\Delta p_{ij} \\): Pressure drop in pipe from node \\(i\\) to \\(j\\) [Pa]\n  - \\( Q_{ij} \\): Volumetric flow rate [m\u00b3/s]\n  - \\( T_{ij} \\): Average pipe temperature [\u00b0C]\n  - \\( \\lambda_{ij} \\): Friction factor [-]\n  - \\( L_{ij} \\): Pipe length [m]\n  - \\( D_{ij} \\): Pipe diameter [m]\n  - \\( \\rho(T) \\): Fluid density at temperature \\(T\\) [kg/m\u00b3]\n  - \\( A_{ij} \\): Cross-sectional area [m\u00b2]\n\n  Empirical functions for \\( \\rho(T) \\) and heat capacity \\( c_p(T) \\) are interpolated from standard tables (see [2], Appendix Sec. S1).\n\n- **Thermal energy balances:** At node \\(k\\):  \n  \\[\n  \\sum_{\\text{inlets}} \\dot{m}_{\\ell}^{\\text{in}} h(T_{\\ell}^{\\text{in}}) = \\sum_{\\text{outlets}} \\dot{m}_{m}^{\\text{out}} h(T_{m}^{\\text{out}}) + Q_{\\text{demand}, k}\n  \\]\n  where:\n  - \\( \\dot{m} \\): Mass flow rate [kg/s]\n  - \\( h(T) = c_p(T) \\cdot T \\): Specific enthalpy [J/kg]\n  - \\( Q_{\\text{demand}, k} \\): Thermal demand at node \\(k\\) [W]\n\n  Temperature mixing constraints ensure that nodes where multiple inlets combine have uniquely defined mixing temperatures, determined by mass and enthalpy conservation ([3], Sec. 4.2).\n\n- **Operational bounds:** Lower and upper bounds are strictly enforced for mass flow rates (\\( \\dot{m}_{\\min}, \\dot{m}_{\\max} \\)), pipe temperatures (\\( T_{\\min}, T_{\\max} \\)), and nodal pressures, with all units and ranges listed in Table S1 (Appendix Sec. S2).\n\n**Steady-State Assumption\u2014Quantitative Applicability:**  \nHydraulic and thermal settling times in district heating networks can be estimated as \\(\\tau_h \\approx V_{\\text{net}}/\\dot{V}_{\\text{main}}\\) and \\(\\tau_t \\approx L_{\\text{max}}/v_{\\text{flow}}\\), where \\(V_{\\text{net}}\\) is total system volume, \\(\\dot{V}_{\\text{main}}\\) is main supply flow, \\(L_{\\text{max}}\\) is maximum pipe length, and \\(v_{\\text{flow}}\\) is flow velocity. For typical networks (\\(V_{\\text{net}}=10^4-10^5\\) m\u00b3, \\(\\dot{V}_{\\text{main}}=0.5-2\\) m\u00b3/s), hydraulic times are 1\u20136 hours. Thermal fronts propagate at similar time scales. Based on [3] and industry data, steady-state models are accurate (error <2%) for time steps \\(\\Delta t \\geq 10 \\tau_h\\), i.e., 1\u20132 hours. For diameters \\(D < 50\\) mm or time-varying loads exceeding \u00b140%/hr, transient effects may induce errors >10%. Users should check these ratios (see Appendix Eq. S2 for calculation steps). Table S2 summarizes tested parameter thresholds. For rapid control events or storage-limited segments, time-resolved (dynamic) analysis is recommended.\n\n**Algorithmic Methodology:**  \nTo address the challenge of global optimization in large, nonlinear networks, we introduce the following methods, each described here with procedural specifics (visualizations and pseudocode are provided in Appendix Fig. S1\u2013S3 and Algorithms S1\u2013S2):\n\n1. **Cyclic Flow Exclusion:**  \n   - Identify network fundamental cycles via graph-theoretic algorithms (e.g., Paton\u2019s algorithm).\n   - For each hydraulic cycle, designate flow directions and designate one dependent edge variable.\n   - Eliminate one variable per cycle by expressing its flow as a function of others (Kirchhoff\u2019s law), thereby reducing the number of free variables and infeasible cycle states.\n   - Pseudocode given in Algorithm S1.\n\n2. **Supply-Return Symmetry Exploitation:**  \n   - Apply an efficient graph automorphism detection algorithm (e.g., using the NAUTY toolkit, [4]) to uncover subgraph symmetries.\n   - When identical subnetworks exist (e.g., duplicated branches or customer clusters), identify equivalent variable permutations.\n   - During branch-and-bound, prune symmetric branches via canonical labeling, avoiding redundant computation.\n\n3. **Temperature Mixing Constraint Reduction:**  \n   - Formulate the nodal mixing matrix and compute its rank.\n   - Remove linearly dependent (redundant) mixing equations via Gaussian elimination.\n   - Only essential mixing constraints are retained per node, which decreases the constraint set size while preserving solution uniqueness.\n\nOn average, these techniques lead to a >50% reduction in branch-and-bound node count (detailed per-case in Table 1 and Appendix Table S3); see Figure S1 for flowchart and algorithm illustration.\n\n**Primal Heuristics:**  \n- **Initialization:** Solve a linearized surrogate of the system\u2014neglecting pressure/temperature nonlinearities\u2014to obtain a feasible starting solution (procedure in Algorithm S2).\n- **Projection-Based Refinement:** Iteratively project onto the feasible set, using the hydraulic/thermal constraint Jacobian and cost gradient, to refine candidate solutions for faster branch-and-bound progress.\n\nBranching priorities are set by ranking nodes and variables by their contributions to temperature deviation and operational cost, as computed from the relaxed solution.\n\n**Experimental Design and Reproducibility:**  \nTesting is conducted on:\n\n- **Synthetic Benchmark Generation:** 100 networks (20\u201350 nodes, 30\u2013100 edges, up to 4 independent loops) are generated by:\n  - Random sampling of nodal coordinates (uniform in a 2 km \u00d7 2 km grid).\n  - Minimum spanning tree connectivity, with \\(1-4\\) random cycle edges added per instance.\n  - Pipe diameters and lengths drawn uniformly from [50, 350] mm and [100, 2000] m, respectively.\n  - Supply and return nodes randomly assigned, each instance tested over 10 random load/temperature profiles (\\(T_{supply} \\in [80, 110]\\)\u00b0C, \\(T_{return} \\in [35, 50]\\)\u00b0C, demand per node uniformly from [30, 300] kW).\n  - All distributions and generation code are released (https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.11019271).\n\n- **Real Danish Networks:**  \n  Three anonymized operational networks (32\u201378 nodes, 2\u20135 loops, max pipeline distance 5 km), with raw structural data and loads provided in [5] and at the same Zenodo link (Appendix Sec. S4 contains schema and metadata).\n\nConsistent operational limits applied: \\(\\dot{m}_{\\min}=0.1\\) kg/s, \\(\\dot{m}_{\\max}=2.5\\) kg/s, \\(T_{\\min}=5\\)\u00b0C, \\(T_{\\max}=120\\)\u00b0C. Solver parameters, including Bonmin and SQP settings, are detailed in Table S1 (Appendix Sec. S2, hyperlinks included for digital readers).\n\n**Performance Results, Uncertainty, and Limitations:**  \nSolvers are compared under identical criteria ("}}